8417	What are text filters and text formats?		11.3.2
8419	Text fields that have "formatted" in the field type name, such as <em>Text (formatted)</em>, use text formats. Users choose the text format when editing the field text; when the field text is shown on the site, it is processed by the chosen text format. Administrators can configure text formats and assign permissions for who can use each format. If the core Text Editor module is enabled, administrators can also associate visual editors with text formats.		11.3.2
8420	What text filters are available?		11.3.2
8421	Some of the more commonly used text filters are:		11.3.2
8422	Limits which HTML tags can be used; useful both for site security and enforcing site design.		11.3.2
8423	Line breaks in HTML source are displayed as horizontal spaces. This filter converts line breaks into HTML paragraph and line break tags.		11.3.2
8424	Takes plain URLs in text and turns them into HTML links.		11.3.2
8425	For text formats that allow HTML image tags, restricts images to URLs within this site.		11.3.2
8426	Click <em>Configure</em> to configure an existing text format, or <em>+ Add text format</em> to create a new text format.		11.3.2
8427	Enter the desired <em>Name</em> for the text format.		11.3.2
8484	<em>No results behavior</em>: what to do if the filter criteria result in having no data to display.		11.3.2
8854	Posting comments		11.3.2
8428	Check the <em>Roles</em> that can use this text format. Some HTML tags allow users to embed malicious links or scripts in text. To ensure security, anonymous and untrusted users should only have access to text formats that restrict them to either plain text or a safe set of HTML tags. <strong>Improper text format configuration is a security risk.</strong>		11.3.2
